1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,783
A back-end job has no one to sign in for it.

2
00:00:03,783 --> 00:00:07,846
With the client credentials flow, it proves who it is with a client

3
00:00:07,846 --> 00:00:11,353
ID and a secret, then calls the Orchard Core Content API.

4
00:00:11,353 --> 00:00:14,080
Let's prepare the site and write the calls.

5
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:24,398
A scheduled import, a build step, or another service runs without a person at the keyboard.

6
00:00:24,398 --> 00:00:28,348
It cannot type a password, so the program signs in as itself:

7
00:00:28,348 --> 00:00:31,590
as an application that you register on your site.

8
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:37,139
The program sends its client ID and client secret to the token endpoint,

9
00:00:37,139 --> 00:00:39,292
and receives an access token.

10
00:00:39,292 --> 00:00:44,460
It then sends that token as a bearer token with every call to the Content API.

11
00:00:45,210 --> 00:00:49,277
The roles you give the application decide what it can do, in addition

12
00:00:49,277 --> 00:00:52,747
to the Authenticated role that every signed-in caller has.

13
00:00:52,747 --> 00:00:57,410
The permissions of the application's roles are copied into the access token when

14
00:00:57,410 --> 00:00:58,820
the token is issued.

15
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:06,242
Under Tools, Features, let's search for OpenID.

16
00:01:06,242 --> 00:01:11,378
Enable OpenID Connect Authorization Server, the feature that issues the tokens.

17
00:01:11,378 --> 00:01:14,580
The features it depends on are enabled with it.

18
00:01:15,046 --> 00:01:18,308
Then enable OpenID Connect Token Validation.

19
00:01:18,308 --> 00:01:23,396
The Content API uses it to check the token of every request it receives.

20
00:01:23,851 --> 00:01:29,991
Under Settings, OpenID Connect, Authorization Server, Enable Token Endpoint is checked.

21
00:01:29,991 --> 00:01:35,801
Check Allow Client Credentials Flow, which stays off until you allow it, and save.

22
00:01:36,253 --> 00:01:43,126
Under Settings, OpenID Connect, Token Validation, the authorization server tenant is the tenant of this site.

23
00:01:43,126 --> 00:01:46,473
The site accepts the tokens that it issues itself.

24
00:01:50,675 --> 00:01:53,798
Under Access Control, Roles, choose Add Role.

25
00:01:53,798 --> 00:01:58,881
Name it Content API Client, a dedicated role that limits the application, and

26
00:01:58,881 --> 00:02:02,005
use Save & Configure to open its permissions.

27
00:02:02,458 --> 00:02:07,410
Search the permissions for api, and allow Access content via the api.

28
00:02:07,410 --> 00:02:12,758
Then search for publish, and allow Publish or unpublish content for others.

29
00:02:13,219 --> 00:02:18,231
Publishing for others includes editing and viewing, so these two permissions are enough

30
00:02:18,231 --> 00:02:20,441
to read, create and update content.

31
00:02:20,441 --> 00:02:21,519
Save the role.

32
00:02:25,724 --> 00:02:31,110
Under Access Control, OpenID Connect, Applications, choose New application.

33
00:02:31,110 --> 00:02:35,564
The form starts with a display name, a type, and a client ID.

34
00:02:36,015 --> 00:02:41,085
Enter Content Sync Job as the Display Name, and content-sync as the Client Id.

35
00:02:41,085 --> 00:02:45,914
Confidential client is selected, so the application needs a Client Secret.

36
00:02:45,914 --> 00:02:47,845
Enter a long random value.

37
00:02:48,296 --> 00:02:51,474
Under Flows, check Allow Client Credentials Flow.

38
00:02:51,474 --> 00:02:55,115
Only the flows that the server settings allow are listed.

39
00:02:55,115 --> 00:02:57,946
A list of Client Credentials Roles appears.

40
00:02:58,403 --> 00:03:01,634
Check the role Content API Client, and save.

41
00:03:01,634 --> 00:03:05,963
The application appears in the list, ready to request tokens.

42
00:03:10,163 --> 00:03:14,486
In the client program, post a form to connect slash token on the site.

43
00:03:14,486 --> 00:03:18,978
It holds the grant type client credentials, the client ID, and the client

44
00:03:18,978 --> 00:03:22,333
secret, which the program reads from its environment.

45
00:03:23,083 --> 00:03:28,275
The response is JSON, and its access underscore token property holds the token.

46
00:03:28,275 --> 00:03:32,307
Keep the secret out of source code and logs, since it is the

47
00:03:32,307 --> 00:03:34,383
password of the application.

48
00:03:38,583 --> 00:03:43,328
Send the access token in the Authorization header as a bearer token.

49
00:03:43,328 --> 00:03:47,753
Then post a content item as JSON to api slash content, with its

50
00:03:47,753 --> 00:03:49,869
content type and its title.

51
00:03:49,869 --> 00:03:52,433
The item is created and published.

52
00:03:53,183 --> 00:03:58,940
The content item properties, such as ContentType and ContentItemId, are case sensitive, so

53
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:00,499
write them as shown.

54
00:04:00,499 --> 00:04:04,816
A body with an existing content item ID updates that item, and the

55
00:04:04,816 --> 00:04:09,193
query string draft equals true saves a draft instead of publishing.

56
00:04:09,943 --> 00:04:13,991
To read an item, send a get to api slash content, a slash,

57
00:04:13,991 --> 00:04:15,888
and the content item ID.

58
00:04:15,888 --> 00:04:20,568
A delete to the same address removes it, but it needs Delete content

59
00:04:20,568 --> 00:04:23,603
for others, which this role does not have.

60
00:04:24,353 --> 00:04:29,449
Every call needs Access content via the api, plus a permission for its action:

61
00:04:29,449 --> 00:04:34,423
View all content to read, Publish or unpublish content for others to create,

62
00:04:34,423 --> 00:04:39,093
Edit content for others to update, Delete content for others to delete.

63
00:04:43,306 --> 00:04:47,044
After the program has run, open Content, Content Items.

64
00:04:47,044 --> 00:04:51,640
The article Hello from the sync job is listed, created by the program

65
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:53,846
through the API and published.

66
00:04:54,297 --> 00:04:58,262
Open the article, and its title is the one the program sent.

67
00:04:58,262 --> 00:05:02,887
From here, an editor can continue working on it like any other article.

68
00:05:07,087 --> 00:05:11,054
An unauthorized response means the request has no accepted token:

69
00:05:11,054 --> 00:05:15,077
check that Token Validation is enabled, and request another token.

70
00:05:15,077 --> 00:05:19,827
A forbidden response means the token is valid, but its role lacks a permission.

71
00:05:20,577 --> 00:05:23,353
A missing content item answers not found.

72
00:05:23,353 --> 00:05:27,665
A body without a known content type answers bad request, and a body

73
00:05:27,665 --> 00:05:31,977
that fails validation answers with the names of the invalid fields.

74
00:05:32,727 --> 00:05:36,530
A change to a role does not reach tokens that were already issued.

75
00:05:36,530 --> 00:05:39,857
Request another token to pick up the changed permissions.

76
00:05:40,607 --> 00:05:45,212
If the token request itself is refused, check that Allow Client Credentials Flow

77
00:05:45,212 --> 00:05:49,282
is checked in the server settings and in the application, and that the

78
00:05:49,282 --> 00:05:51,477
client ID and the secret are right.

79
00:05:53,477 --> 00:05:55,973
That is the client credentials flow:

80
00:05:55,973 --> 00:06:00,786
a registered application, a role that limits it, an access token, and calls

81
00:06:00,786 --> 00:06:02,272
to the Content API.

82
00:06:02,272 --> 00:06:03,817
Thanks for watching.

